explanation:
The issue arises from attempting to access elements of an array that has null values without checking for nullity first. In the given code, when `array[i]` is accessed in the for loop, it tries to call `getClass()` on the value stored at index `i`. If this value is `null`, calling `getClass()` will result in a `NullPointerException`. To fix this issue, you should check if `array[i]` is not null before attempting to call `getClass()` on its value. Here's how it can be done:

```
for (int i = 0; i < array.length; i++) {
    if (array[i] != null) { 
        classes[i] = ClassUtils.toClass(array[i]);
    } else { 
        classes[i] = null;
    }
}
```
This way, you ensure that `getClass()` is only called on non-null values, avoiding the `NullPointerException`. Additionally, when encountering a null value in the array, you set `classes[i]` to `null`, as expected.
